            <fr:title text="Greedy Parsing">Greedy Parsing</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
  A regular expression <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> may be unambiguous. To resolve this unambiguity one may choose to instead replace <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> with an unambiguous goal for parsing, or instead one may define a disambiguation strategy for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
For instance, in <fr:link href="/frischCardelli/" title="Greedy regular expression matching" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/frischCardelli/" display-uri="frischCardelli" type="local">Greedy regular expression matching</fr:link> Frisch and Cardelli alleviate the ambiguity of parsing a Kleene star by always taking the <html:code>cons</html:code> branch when possible. That is, when scanning over a string left-to-right with the goal of parsing a Kleene star of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and then another grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>, we <html:em>greedily</html:em> parse a Kleene star of maximal length before moving on to a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>This same greediness approach is common in parsing theory. It is also used in <fr:link href="/henglein_regular_2011/" title="Regular expression containment: Coinductive axiomatization and computational interpretation" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/henglein_regular_2011/" display-uri="henglein_regular_2011" type="local">Regular expression containment: Coinductive axiomatization and computational interpretation</fr:link> and in parsing expression grammars.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
